# Force text treatment and LF endings on source.
#
# Why this exists: a scripted edit once wrote two NUL bytes into src/db/stock.ts. The file
# was cleaned in a19e5d2, but git heuristically treats a path as binary if any blob in the
# comparison contains a NUL — so `git diff` and `git show` rendered
# "Bin 3728 -> 5294 bytes" for the file holding the weighted-average arithmetic. Losing a
# readable diff on the costing code is a review problem, not a cosmetic one.
#
# `diff` (bare) is the attribute that forces a TEXT diff - `text` alone only governs
# checkin/checkout normalisation, so git still sniffed the NUL and called it binary. `eol=lf` also settles the CRLF warnings this repo emits
# on Windows: the working tree matches what is stored, so diffs stay honest.
#
# See src/lib/source-integrity.test.ts, which fails the build if a NUL ever returns.

*.ts   text eol=lf diff
*.tsx  text eol=lf diff
*.mjs  text eol=lf
*.js   text eol=lf
*.css  text eol=lf diff=css
*.json text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf diff=markdown
*.sql  text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Anything that RUNS ON THE LINUX SERVER. Without these, core.autocrlf=true on a Windows
# checkout writes them with CRLF - and deploy/README.md ships them to the VPS by scp FROM
# that checkout. A shell script with CRLF fails as `/bin/sh^M: bad interpreter`, and
# backup.sh runs under cron, so it would fail silently every night into a log nobody
# reads: the backup that quietly stops is the one failure a backup exists to prevent.
# The committed blobs were always LF; only the working copy was wrong, which is why CI
# never saw it.
*.sh       text eol=lf
Caddyfile  text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Real binaries: never diff or normalise these.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip  binary
